      "risk_exposure": "not_demonstrated",
      "recommended_risk": "low",
      "technical_explanation": "The repeated signal corresponds to several near-identical block-stripping regular expressions that match a labeled header line followed by a lazily repeated single-line group with a lookahead, used to remove engine-internal blocks from the assembled system message before prompting. Although static analyzers flag the nested repetition shape as a potential runaway-backtracking risk, the dot class here excludes line terminators, so each repetition consumes exactly one line and the lookahead's end-of-line alternative is satisfied at the next line break under multiline mode. Matching is therefore effectively linear over the prompt-sized text it processes, and no catastrophic backtracking path is demonstrated on realistic input. Even in a worst case, the outcome would be a transient slowdown of a single generation inside the user's own tab, which is a low-impact quality issue rather than a security exposure. The same function also builds a regular expression from strip patterns supplied by the active lore module, but lore modules are user-installed code that already runs arbitrary logic, so that path grants no additional capability.",
      "layman_explanation": "A scanner flagged some text-cleaning patterns as potentially slow. Reading the actual code, these patterns strip labeled blocks of text one line at a time and are very unlikely to cause the slowdowns the scanner worries about. Even in a bad case, the only effect would be a briefly slower response on the user's own machine, so this is a code-quality nit rather than a security problem.",
      "developer_action": "Optional cleanup: replace the repeated lookahead-based block strippers with straightforward line-by-line parsing to remove the nested-repetition shape and the scanner signal. No security change is required.",
